<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Patch>

	<!-- Enrols the red wolf in A Dog Said... Animal Prosthetics 2.

	     THIS FILE REPLACES PAUSBRAK'S ORIGINAL, WHICH NO LONGER DID ANYTHING. It named
	     thirteen recipes one by one - InstallPegLegAnimal, InstallBionicEyeAnimal,
	     InstallBionicAnimalStomach and the rest - and guarded on the display name of
	     A Dog Said 1. ADS 2 renamed every one of those recipes, and ADS 1 is dead. Both
	     halves missed: the guard never matched, and had it matched, the xpath would have
	     selected nothing.

	     ADS 2 does not name recipes one by one. It keeps three abstract RecipeDefs -
	     ADS_Cat1 (medieval replacements), ADS_Cat2 (+ simple prosthetics) and ADS_Cat3
	     (+ bionics) - and its own last patch copies their recipeUsers onto the real surgery
	     bases. Adding an animal means adding one <li> to those lists, which is what every
	     ModCompat file in ADS 2 does.

	     All three, because the red wolf is a variant of the timber wolf, and Wolf_Timber
	     sits in all three of ADS 2's lists. The lists are cumulative.

	     The guard is PatchOperationConditional on ADS_Cat1. Without ADS 2 the xpath selects
	     nothing, no <nomatch> is declared, and the operation reports success in silence.
	     Not MayRequire: on an <Operation> that attribute is read by nothing, and the
	     operation would apply either way.

	     Every branch repeats @Name=. The form [@Name="A" or "B"] is not the test it looks
	     like - XPath converts each operand of `or` to a boolean, and a non-empty string
	     literal is always true, so such a predicate matches every RecipeDef in the document.

	     About.xml declares <loadBefore> on ADS 2: its category patch runs once, at its own
	     pass, so a mod loaded after it writes into a list nobody reads again. -->
	<Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
		<xpath>/Defs/RecipeDef[@Name="ADS_Cat1"]</xpath>
		<match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
			<xpath>/Defs/RecipeDef[
				@Name="ADS_Cat3" or
				@Name="ADS_Cat2" or
				@Name="ADS_Cat1"
			]/recipeUsers</xpath>
			<value>
				<li>Wolf_Red</li>
			</value>
		</match>
	</Operation>

</Patch>
